Created the Testimonial slice's React component at `slices/Testimonial/index.jsx`:

```jsx
import { PrismicRichText } from "@prismicio/react";
import { PrismicNextImage } from "@prismicio/next";

const Testimonial = ({ slice }) => {
	return (
		<section
			data-slice-type={slice.slice_type}
			data-slice-variation={slice.variation}
		>
			<PrismicNextImage field={slice.primary.avatar} />
			<PrismicRichText field={slice.primary.quote} />
			<p>{slice.primary.attribution}</p>
		</section>
	);
};

export default Testimonial;
```

What I did along the way, in order:
1. The model already existed locally (`slices/Testimonial/model.json`) but wasn't yet in Prismic or committed to git, so I committed it, then ran `npx prismic push` to create it on the repository.
2. The project had never been scaffolded for Prismic (no `@prismicio/*` deps, no client file), so I ran `npx prismic gen setup`, which added the dependencies to `package.json` and generated `prismicio.js`, the slice simulator page, and preview/revalidate API routes.
3. Ran `npx prismic pull` to sync — but the CLI only auto-generates a starter component when a slice is *newly inserted*; since this slice already existed locally, pull treated it as an update and skipped component bootstrapping. So I wrote the component by hand, following the exact convention the CLI itself uses for Next.js slices (confirmed from its source), then filled in the real implementation using the slice's three fields (`quote` rich text, `avatar` image, `attribution` text) instead of the placeholder markup.

Note: `npm install` reported "added 0 packages" (likely no network access in this sandbox) — `@prismicio/client`, `@prismicio/react`, and `@prismicio/next` are listed in `package.json` but not yet present in `node_modules`, so run `npm install` before using the component. Also, `<PrismicPreview>` still needs to be added to `app/layout.jsx` manually, as the setup command noted.

